
3 mutually exclusive primary value categories: lvalue, prvalue, xvalue


rvalue   an expression that does not have an explicit memory location

prvalue  (pure rvalue) is defined as an expression whose evaluation
         satisfies one of two outcomes: 1) It computes the value of an operand
         of a built-in operator (this computation does not denote an
         object). 2) It initializes an object (or a bit-field).

         A prvalue is an expression whose evaluation either computes the
         value of an operand or initializes an object.
         a prvalue has no identity; it does not have a permanent
         memory address that you can access via the address-of operator
         (&)—but its resources can be moved

xvalue  (expiring value)
glvalue (generalized lvalue) an lvalue or an xvalue



